---
import ArticleLayout from '../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: "intro", label: "What is a vector?" },
  { id: "notation", label: "Notation & components" },
  { id: "ops", label: "Basic operations" },
  { id: "dot", label: "Dot product" },
  { id: "cross", label: "Cross product" },
  { id: "norms", label: "Norms, unit vectors & projection" },
  { id: "identities", label: "Useful identities" },
  { id: "exercises", label: "Exercises" },
];
---

<ArticleLayout subject="maths" topic="vectors" toc={toc}>
  <!--
    is:raw stops Astro reading the braces in LaTeX as template expressions.
    The classes available for this content are listed at the top of public/styles.css.
  -->
  <Fragment is:raw>
    <!-- 1. Intro -->
    <section id="intro" class="chapter">
      <h2>1. What is a vector?</h2>
      <p>
        A vector represents a quantity with <em>magnitude</em> and <em>direction</em>. In $\mathbb{R}^n$ we write
        $\mathbf{v} = (v_1,\dots,v_n)$, and its length (Euclidean norm) is
        $\lVert \mathbf{v} \rVert = \sqrt{v_1^2+\cdots+v_n^2}$.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 2. Notation & components -->
    <section id="notation" class="chapter">
      <h2>2. Notation & components</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Vectors in <strong>bold</strong> (e.g., $\mathbf{v}$); scalars in roman (e.g., $a$).</li>
        <li>2D: $\mathbf{v}=(v_x, v_y)$; 3D: $\mathbf{v}=(v_x, v_y, v_z)$ with basis $\{\mathbf{i},\mathbf{j},\mathbf{k}\}$.</li>
        <li>Unit vector: $\hat{\mathbf{v}} = \dfrac{\mathbf{v}}{\lVert \mathbf{v} \rVert}$ (if $\mathbf{v}\neq \mathbf{0}$).</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 3. Basic operations -->
    <section id="ops" class="chapter">
      <h2>3. Basic operations</h2>
      <p><strong>Addition:</strong> $\ \mathbf{u}+\mathbf{v} = (u_x+v_x,\, u_y+v_y,\, u_z+v_z)$.</p>
      <p><strong>Scalar multiplication:</strong> $\ a\mathbf{v} = (a v_x,\, a v_y,\, a v_z)$ for $a\in\mathbb{R}$.</p>
      <p><strong>Magnitude:</strong> $\ \lVert \mathbf{v} \rVert = \sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2+v_z^2}$.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 4. Dot product -->
    <section id="dot" class="chapter">
      <h2>4. Dot product</h2>
      <p>
        Algebraic: $\ \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v} = u_x v_x + u_y v_y + u_z v_z = \sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.$
        Geometric: $\ \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v} = \lVert \mathbf{u} \rVert \,\lVert \mathbf{v} \rVert \cos\theta$,
        where $\theta$ is the angle between $\mathbf{u}$ and $\mathbf{v}$.
      </p>
      <p>Consequences: Cauchy–Schwarz $\ \lvert \mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}\rvert \le \lVert \mathbf{u}\rVert \,\lVert \mathbf{v}\rVert$ and orthogonality when $\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}=0$.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. Cross product -->
    <section id="cross" class="chapter">
      <h2>5. Cross product (3D)</h2>
      <p>
        The cross product $\ \mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v}$ is a vector orthogonal to both, with magnitude
        $\lVert \mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v} \rVert = \lVert \mathbf{u}\rVert \,\lVert \mathbf{v}\rVert \sin\theta$
        and direction given by the right-hand rule.
      </p>
      <p>
        Component form: 
      </p>
      <p>
        $$\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v} =
        \begin{vmatrix}
          \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
          u_x & u_y & u_z \\
          v_x & v_y & v_z
        \end{vmatrix}
        =
        \big(u_y v_z - u_z v_y\big)\mathbf{i}
        - \big(u_x v_z - u_z v_x\big)\mathbf{j}
        + \big(u_x v_y - u_y v_x\big)\mathbf{k}.$$
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 6. Norms, unit vectors & projection -->
    <section id="norms" class="chapter">
      <h2>6. Norms, unit vectors & projection</h2>
      <p>
        Normalize: $\ \hat{\mathbf{v}} = \dfrac{\mathbf{v}}{\lVert\mathbf{v}\rVert}$.
        Projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto $\mathbf{u}\neq\mathbf{0}$:
        $$\mathrm{proj}_{\mathbf{u}} \mathbf{v} \;=\; \frac{\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}}{\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{u}}\,\mathbf{u}
        \;=\; \frac{\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}}{\lVert \mathbf{u}\rVert^2}\,\mathbf{u}.$$
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 7. Useful identities -->
    <section id="identities" class="chapter">
      <h2>7. Useful identities</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Distributivity: $\ \mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}+\mathbf{c})=\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}+\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{c}$, and similarly for $\times$.</li>
        <li>Triple product: $\ \mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})$ equals the signed volume; cyclic permutations preserve value (up to sign).</li>
        <li>Vector triple product: $\ \mathbf{a}\times(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})=\mathbf{b}(\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{c})-\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b})$.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- 8. Exercises -->
    <section id="exercises" class="chapter">
      <h2>8. Exercises</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Find the angle between $\mathbf{u}=(2,-1,2)$ and $\mathbf{v}=(1,2,2)$.</li>
        <li>Compute $\mathrm{proj}_{\mathbf{u}}\mathbf{v}$ for $\mathbf{u}=(3,0,4)$ and $\mathbf{v}=(2,1,-1)$.</li>
        <li>Show that $\lVert \mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v}\rVert^2 = \lVert \mathbf{u}\rVert^2 \lVert \mathbf{v}\rVert^2 - (\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v})^2$.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
